{
  "dom_id": "step-q113899",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/42fd32f22d25ed1d26d48ca8d4f1f5f454bcca67c68febaec066fa3b8bde0783.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave down *and* ${f}^{\u2032}(x)<0?$",
  "worked_solution": "We begin by noticing that at $x=a$ the graph has a corner point, which implies that the derivative doesn't exist there.\n\nThe derivative ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ is negative when $f(x)$ is decreasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=b$ and $x=c$ are on decreasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/f6e6c7b869268eba113690776b0aa07713ff12adb675e22e11406c4a13f0e7b2.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\u2323$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\u2322$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=b$ and $x=c,$ the graph is concave down ($\u2322$) at $x=c$ only.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
